Electrical discharge machining is frequently adopted for difficult-to-machine nickel‑chromium alloys such as Hastelloy C‑22 because conventional machining is often ineffective for these materials. {{cite:S1#c1}} The physical and mechanical properties of the tool electrode directly influence process efficiency, machining accuracy, and surface roughness in EDM of such alloys. {{cite:S1#c1}} Recent studies report a marked trend toward using graphite electrodes, motivated by low raw-material cost, good machinability, and high sublimation temperature. {{cite:S1#c1}}

The two recent experimental studies on graphite electrodes report some agreement but also clear differences. Both studies emphasise the importance of electrode properties and machining parameters for MRR, TWR and Ra and both investigate POCO graphite grades with 1 µm and 10 µm grain sizes. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}} Nowicki et al. (2022) found that larger-grain graphite provides higher process efficiency (higher MRR) but at the cost of higher relative tool wear, while the smallest surface roughness was obtained with the finer-grained graphite; discharge current and pulse duration were identified as the main factors for MRR and Ra, and the time interval dominated relative tool wear. {{cite:S1#c1}} Nowicki et al. (2024), by contrast, report that fine-grained graphite increases process efficiency and reduces tool wear, and they highlight the effect of detached graphite grains on discharge stability and process efficiency. {{cite:S3#c1}} Thus the studies agree that grain size affects MRR, TWR and Ra and that machining parameters strongly influence outcomes, but they differ on whether larger or finer graphite grain yields the best combination of high efficiency and low tool wear. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}

Additional EDM studies support the importance of current and pulse duration: Nas & Kara found peak current to be the most influential factor for Ra and MRR, {{cite:S2#c1}} and Ramabalan et al. observed that current and pulse duration greatly affect electrode wear and surface edges. {{cite:S4#c1}} These findings align with the two graphite studies in identifying electrical parameters as primary drivers of machining performance. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}

