Electrical discharge machining is frequently adopted for difficult-to-machine nickel‑chromium alloys such as Hastelloy C‑22 because conventional machining is often ineffective for these materials. {{cite:S1#c1}} The physical and mechanical properties of the tool electrode directly influence process efficiency, machining accuracy, and surface roughness in EDM of such alloys. {{cite:S1#c1}} Recent studies report a marked trend toward using graphite electrodes, motivated by low raw-material cost, good machinability, and high sublimation temperature. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Nowicki et al. (2022) report that graphite with a larger grain provides higher process efficiency but with higher relative tool wear, and that the lowest surface roughness was obtained for finer-grained graphite. {{cite:S1#c1}} By contrast, Nowicki et al. (2024) state that fine-grained graphite electrodes increase process efficiency and reduce tool wear. {{cite:S3#c1}} Thus the two Nowicki studies differ on which graphite grain size simultaneously optimizes efficiency and tool wear (S1 reports higher efficiency with larger grains but higher wear; S3 reports improved efficiency and reduced wear with fine grains). {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}

Several other studies agree with the emphasis on machining parameters: Nas & Kara identify peak current (and pulse-on time) as the most influential factors for MRR and Ra in their Taguchi/ANOVA analysis. {{cite:S2#c1}} Ramabalan et al. likewise report that current and pulse duration greatly affect electrode wear and machined edges. {{cite:S4#c1}} More broadly, reviews note the persistent challenges of high tool wear rate, low material removal rate, and high surface roughness in EDM and report various combined techniques proposed to mitigate them. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Household rooftop solar adoption literature consistently identifies upfront cost and affordability as primary barriers to uptake. Several studies report that high initial investment deters households and that government subsidies or flexible payment options are central to overcoming this barrier. Parsad et al. find that government subsidies act as a primary motivator by helping to overcome the initial investment risk in rooftop PV adoption, and that low prices and low maintenance costs further mitigate long-term apprehension about returns and upkeep. {{cite:S3#c1}} Tanveer et al. likewise show perceived cost has a negative effect on consumers' willingness to adopt household solar PV in Pakistan, and they additionally find perceived risk reduces willingness while social norms and self-efficacy increase it. {{cite:S6#c1}} 

Comparing Parsad et al. and Tanveer et al.: both identify cost-related factors as important inhibitors of household adoption and indicate that reducing perceived financial risk should increase uptake. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S6#c1}} They differ in emphasis beyond cost—Parsad et al. highlight government subsidies and also rank environmental motives and technical/awareness factors as significant motivators or predictors in their ANN and SEM analyses, {{cite:S3#c1}} whereas Tanveer et al. emphasize social and behavioral determinants (social norms, self-efficacy) alongside perceived risk and find openness to technology to be insignificant. {{cite:S6#c1}} 

Aarakit et al.'s findings on affordability and flexible payment mechanisms are cited here but the source text for that study is not provided in the passages. [[NEEDS INPUT: Provide passage S7 for Aarakit et al.]]
